> Exact-level ties, finite range and complete contracts

Because equality is above, a corner exactly on the level can make two crossings
coincide. A zero-length segment is retained rather than silently deduplicated;
neighboring cells can also produce duplicate endpoints or pieces. Returned
segments have no global stitching, contour orientation or simple-loop guarantee.
A constant cell entirely equal to the level has case15 and emits nothing under
this convention, despite the full cell being part of that mathematical level
set.

For finite ordinary edge differences the historical interpolation arithmetic is
retained. If opposite full-range samples make raw differences nonfinite,
positive common scaling computes a finite between-endpoints fraction. This is a
finite arithmetic fallback, not an exact location certificate. Complete
finite/ragged/level/rule validation occurs even on empty or no-cell grids, and
checked cell/output geometry precedes extraction. Grid-index coordinates must
remain representable as adjacent exact integers in double.

Each actual cell is visited once and only crossed edges are interpolated, so
validation/extraction is O(rows*columns) plus returned segments. Actual trace
admission counts original scalar samples plus each real cell, crossing and
emitted-segment record; default cap is one million, zero disables only that
optional cap. Fields, row headers, capacities, returned/runtime copies and
physical bytes are excluded. Unknown future cases are not fabricated, and
captures own the complete original grid. Empty, zero-width and one-dimensional
valid grids produce no cells; a radial study with no segments has no midpoint
observations, with mean/max zero explicitly serving only as placeholders.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/marching_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const double m = std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::MarchingSquaresTrace> controls;
    controls.push_back(dm::algorithms::marching_squares_trace(
                       {{0, -1}, {-1, -1}}, 0));
    controls.push_back(dm::algorithms::marching_squares_trace({{1, 1}, {1, 1}},
                                                              1));
    controls.push_back(dm::algorithms::marching_squares_trace(
                       {{-m, m}, {-m, m}}, 0));
    std::vector<double> counts;
    std::print("| prescribed control0:corner-tie,1:constant-level,2:full-range | actual case | actual " "interpolations | actual segments |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < controls.size(); ++i) {
        auto &t = controls[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.cells.front().case_index,
                   t.analysis.work.interpolations, t.analysis.segments.size());
        counts.push_back(t.analysis.segments.size());
    }
    std::print("\n| actual control | actual edge | actual finite fraction | scaled fallback |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < controls.size(); ++i)
        for (auto &c : controls[i].crossings)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, c.edge, c.fraction,
                       c.scaled);
    auto &s = controls[0].analysis.segments.front();
    std::print("\nActual retained corner-tie segment ({},{}) to({},{}).\n",
               s[0], s[1], s[2], s[3]);
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(counts,
                                     {"corner tie", "constant level",
                                      "full-range samples"}, "actual segments",
                                     {37, 99, 235});
    p.x_label("prescribed cell control").y_label("actual emitted segment records");
    p.y_limits(0, 1.6).title("Actual tie/constant/full-range contour controls").save_svg("build/marching_controls.svg");
}
```
