> Verification and references

Native tests exhaust all16 corner masks under both explicit saddle rules, check
exact crossed-edge midpoints and selected pairs, retain the original44-segment
radial field and midpoint observations, verify affine-field crossings and actual
segment spans, and cover exact-level degeneracy, unused constant edges, opposite
maximum samples, complete finite/shape/rule guards, no-cell cases, exact capture
admission and ownership. The ten-report batch passes 95 CTest cases, 30 directly
instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 121 compiled and executed public examples,
601/601 scoped documentation definitions and 11 representative hovers.
Independent integer, Fraction, graph, string and NumPy references verify 82,729
records, including the complete original seeded LSH study. Frozen comparisons
preserve ordinary legacy results; malformed-input, sentinel-collision, ownership
and explicit-budget corrections are documented above. Thirteen checkout headers
and thirteen installed headers compile independently, and an installed-header
native consumer links and runs. Each complete report program runs against the
checkout library; all four native figures are reviewed and HTML/Markdown exports
preserve computed results and image bytes. Batch receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-214-223/`.

G. M. Nielson and B. Hamann(1991),
[The Asymptotic Decider: Resolving the Ambiguity in Marching Cubes|https://doi.org/10.1109/VISUAL.1991.175782],
Proceedings of Visualization,83–91, explains why sampled corner classifications
leave bilinear face connectivity ambiguous. This implementation deliberately
exposes two fixed marching-squares pairings and does not claim to implement that
asymptotic decider.
